          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The net primarily symbolizes the gospel or kingdom gathering people through evangelism, especially the broad ingathering depicted in Matthew's dragnet parable; trap and judgment meanings are context-dependent alternatives.\",\"book_summary\":\"The net symbolizes the gospel's gathering of all kinds of people from the figurative sea of humanity.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Net = the gospel/kingdom's instrument of gathering people.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Net = the gospel's gathering of all kinds of people.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"No material boundary disagreement. The book focuses on Matthew 13's gathering of every kind and omits the consensus's additional discussion of final sorting, apostolic mission, and negative net imagery, but it does not reject those contextual details.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. Both identify the net centrally with gospel or kingdom ingathering.\",\"rationale\":\"Reduced to their minimum cores, both readings identify the net as the gospel or kingdom gathering people. The phrase 'out of the sea of peoples' elaborates the setting rather than replacing the referent. The book's brevity and omission of judgment or alternative biblical uses do not establish a contrary or materially narrower claim.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Matthew 13:47 explicitly compares the kingdom of heaven, not the gospel alone, to the net, and verses 48-50 include eschatological separation. Thus 'gospel' and 'sea of peoples' are interpretive glosses, although the cited verses strongly support the central gathering identification.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
